{
  "rationale": "Evidence from the iteration-2 race (score 735.75, best so far): oval 20/20 (rms 0.0046), s-bend 20/20 (rms 0.010, zero line-loss events), slalom 0/20 (14 off-track exits, 6 timeouts, mean progress 0.74, steering_oscillation 0.34, control_effort 15.4). Iteration 3 widened the sensors to ±0.05 m and raised base_speed to 0.95. That dropped the score to 651.9: the s-bend fell to 17/20 and slalom progress fell from 0.74 to 0.35 with 20/20 off-track. So I am reverting the sensor spread to ±0.04 m and base_speed to 0.9, the best-known settings. The one-variable change is kd 2.0 -> 3.0. Across iterations, raising kd from 1 to 2 cut slalom oscillation and raised progress (0.45 -> 0.74), and the slalom is still the bottleneck. The slalom's high oscillation (0.34) and off-track exits on reversals suggest the reversals still need more damping. The oval (oscillation 0.24) and s-bend have margin, so a modest kd increase should not hurt them. Expected result: slalom progress above 0.85 with fewer off-track exits, and the oval and s-bend still finishing. Hypotheses to check next: (1) if oval steering_oscillation rises above about 0.4 or oval rms_error rises, kd 3 is too high and should drop to about 2.5. (2) If slalom still leaves at similar progress, the limit is sensor geometry or steering authority, so try kp 5 or sensor spacing changes. (3) If the slalom mainly times out (not off-track), base_speed needs to rise slightly, since timeouts mean the robot is too slow. Measurements requested: all metrics per track, so slalom progress, off-track vs timeout counts, and steering_oscillation can be compared directly with iteration 2.",
